---
Scope: .swarm/manifests/S5.6.files. Confirmed comments-only change: attempt 5
vs attempt 6 diff on the two flagged sentences shows only prose edits; no Go
statement, Makefile recipe, or JS execution line changed (checked all ten
manifest files' non-comment lines are unchanged from the S5.5-recorded state
via re-reading handlers.go/handlers_test.go/transfers files, Makefile recipe
lines 77-207, and the harness JS code below its header comment).

7a (stale pointer) and the S5.5 7b (unscoped "every other target") overclaim
are both fixed: the harness header now says "The guard lives in the test
itself, not in any Makefile target," and the package comment scopes the claim
to "any invocation that RUNS this package's tests" with test-integration/fuzz
correctly named as out of scope. `make check` genuinely reaches the guard
(check: vet static vuln test -> test: go test ./...) -- verified, not asserted.

But the new sentence overclaims on a different axis, the one the task flagged:

QUOTED CLAIM (warnings_client_regression_test.go, package comment):
  "even a warm test cache (this test execs node and reads a file outside
   its package directory, so the cache never replays it)"

FALSIFIED. A warm cache DOES replay this test when nothing relevant changed
-- that is the literal, unconditional claim in the parenthetical, and it is
false by direct observation:

  $ go clean -testcache
  $ make test 2>&1 | grep accounts
    ok  	budget2/internal/handlers/accounts	0.585s
    ok  	budget2/internal/services/accounts	0.011s
  $ make test 2>&1 | grep accounts        # unchanged tree, second run
    ok  	budget2/internal/handlers/accounts	(cached)
    ok  	budget2/internal/services/accounts	(cached)

"(cached)" is `go test`'s own literal word for "this package's test result was
served from cache, the binary did not re-run" -- meaning node was NOT
re-invoked and the JS harness did NOT re-execute on the second `make test`.
Reproduced identically with `go test -run TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness
./internal/handlers/accounts/...` run twice back to back (second run: `ok
budget2/internal/handlers/accounts (cached)`), and with an unrelated env var
added (`SOME_UNRELATED_VAR=xyz$$ go test ...` -> still `(cached)`).

Separating the two candidate mechanisms the task asked me to distinguish:

1. "reads a file outside its package directory" -- tested in isolation with a
   throwaway module (test that os.ReadFile's a file one directory above its
   package, no exec at all). Unchanged reruns of that test ALSO show
   `(cached)` once mtimes settle, and mutating the outside file's content DOES
   bust the cache on the next run. This is generic Go test-cache file-content
   tracking (via internal/testlog) that applies to ANY os.Open/os.ReadFile
   target regardless of location -- reading testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js
   (which IS inside this package's directory) would be tracked identically.
   "Outside its package directory" is not what makes the read trackable; it is
   a red herring as a distinguishing explanation.

2. PATH / exec.LookPath -- confirmed this is the piece that responds to "node
   became unavailable": stripping node's directory from PATH busts the cache
   and the test fails loudly (reproduced). Appending a harmless, nonexistent
   directory to PATH (node still resolves to the same binary) ALSO busts the
   cache and forces a real re-run, confirming PATH's string value itself,
   not just node's resolved location, is part of the tracked inputs -- this
   is closer to what actually protects the "node went missing" case, and is
   the attribution the attempt-6 worker used in their own verification per
   the task brief, not what ended up in the shipped comment.

Neither mechanism supports "the cache never replays it" as an absolute
claim -- both are ordinary cache-invalidate-on-input-change behavior, which
by definition means the cache DOES replay when inputs are unchanged, which is
exactly what "even a warm test cache ... hits the guard" was trying to deny.
The parenthetical's literal reading (this specific test's cache entry is
never honored) is directly falsified by `make test` run twice; the more
charitable reading (cache correctly busts when node-availability or the
template content changes) is true but for a different, less specific reason
than the one stated, and "reads a file outside its package directory" adds a
false distinguishing detail that doesn't hold up under a same-mechanism
control read from inside the package directory.

This is the same defect class the task has now failed on for two prior
attempts: a comment asserting more than the code (or in this case, more than
Go's test-cache semantics) actually deliver. The claim is longer and more
specific than attempts 4/5's, and the extra specificity is exactly where it
breaks.

Other angles attacked, all held:
- Opt-out presence-based semantics: `BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=0` and `=false`
  both skip (verified against the code: `os.Getenv(skipEnvVar) != ""`, no
  value-parsing, so any non-empty string including whitespace-only opts out --
  confirmed `BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=" "` with node stripped from PATH produces
  a Skip, matching the comment). README and test-comment wording agree.
- `make check` reaches the guard for a real reason (traced the prerequisite
  chain), not coincidentally.
- test-integration/fuzz correctly listed as not-covered; fuzz's `-run=^$`
  would exclude this test even if a Fuzz func were later added to this
  package, so the "not a hole" framing holds there.
- Did not run .swarm/tier3/S5/accept.sh (checker-tests owns it this round);
  all commands above were constructed independently against the working tree.
- Pre-S5 template regression (md5 fb2a5b02af41e835bbe1fc52fd22ed69,
  authenticate-first) and the subtler partial revert: not re-tested this
